(window.GROUP_QUIZZES=window.GROUP_QUIZZES||{})["clinical-medicine-and-surgery-i-exam-3-neck-masses-neoplasms-vignettes"]={"title":"Neck Masses and Neoplasms Vignettes 1 — CMS I Exam 3","category":"Clinical Medicine and Surgery I","exam":"Exam 3","sem":"fall-2026","questions":[{"q":"A 31-year-old woman's thyroid nodule biopsy shows a well-differentiated carcinoma of thyroid epithelial cells. She is told it is the commonest type with the best outlook.","choices":["Papillary carcinoma","Follicular carcinoma","Medullary carcinoma","Anaplastic carcinoma"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Papillary carcinoma is about 75 per cent of thyroid cancers, arises from thyroid epithelial cells, is commonest in young women and has the best prognosis. It tends to spread to local cervical nodes rather than distantly, which contributes to that outlook. — CMS I Neoplasms and Neck Masses, Slide 42"},{"q":"A 52-year-old man from a high-prevalence region has diffuse bilateral cervical lymphadenopathy with weight loss and night sweats.","choices":["Atypical mycobacterial infection","Reactive viral lymphadenopathy","Actinomycosis","Tuberculous adenitis"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Cervical tuberculosis, called scrofula, affects adults more than children and produces lymphadenopathy that is more diffuse and bilateral than atypical mycobacterial disease. Diagnosis is by tuberculin skin testing with acid-fast stain and culture, and treatment is the RIPE regimen. — CMS I Neoplasms and Neck Masses, Slide 34"},{"q":"A 28-year-old man has a painless firm supraclavicular node that has grown over two months. He has had no infection.","choices":["Lymphoma","Reactive viral adenopathy","Branchial cleft cyst","Thyroid nodule"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Most neck masses under 40 are inflammatory, and Hodgkin lymphoma is the named exception to that rule. A painless firm node enlarging over months without infection is the presentation the exception exists for, and supraclavicular location adds concern because those nodes drain the thorax and abdomen. — CMS I Neoplasms and Neck Masses, Slide 13"},{"q":"A neck mass has failed to settle after antibiotics, and computed tomography shows a solid mass. What comes next?","choices":["Another course of antibiotics","Fine needle aspiration biopsy","Excisional biopsy","Positron emission tomography"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Imaging characterises the mass but cannot give a tissue diagnosis. Aspiration is the standard of care because it separates neoplasm from inflammation and carcinoma from lymphoma, and it is preferred over excision because excision spills tumour and complicates definitive treatment. — CMS I Neoplasms and Neck Masses, Slide 18"},{"q":"A 60-year-old man with a 40 pack-year history has an ulcerated firm neck mass.","choices":["Presume a congenital cyst that has become infected","Presume reactive adenopathy from smoking","Presume malignancy","Presume infection given the ulceration"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Always presume a new neck mass is malignant until proven otherwise in this group. Ulceration is itself on the red-flag list, alongside age over 40, tobacco and alcohol, firmness, immobility, size and duration, and this patient carries several at once. — CMS I Neoplasms and Neck Masses, Slide 39"},{"q":"A 66-year-old man has an asymptomatic, slowly progressive, firm neck mass, with hoarseness and odynophagia.","choices":["A benign soft tissue tumour","An infected congenital cyst","Reactive lymphadenopathy","A malignant neck neoplasm, most likely metastatic squamous carcinoma"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Malignant neck tumours are usually metastatic squamous cell carcinoma from skin or upper aerodigestive tract, and the described pattern is exactly that: asymptomatic lesions that progress slowly and feel firm, with hoarseness, dysphagia and odynophagia reflecting the primary site. — CMS I Neoplasms and Neck Masses, Slide 37"},{"q":"A clinician documents a cervical node as immobile, firm and non-tender.","choices":["Features that raise concern for malignancy","Features typical of an inflammatory node","Features indicating a cystic lesion","Features indicating a vascular lesion"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. The descriptors that matter are size, shape, mobility, consistency and tenderness. Immobility suggests fixation to surrounding structures by tumour, firmness suggests solid tissue rather than fluid, and non-tenderness points away from inflammation, which hurts. — CMS I Neoplasms and Neck Masses, Slide 93"},{"q":"A 49-year-old trumpet player has hoarseness, cough and a foreign body sensation in the throat. Laryngoscopy shows a smooth dilation at the level of the false cord.","choices":["Laryngeal carcinoma","Laryngocele","Vocal cord polyp","Vocal cord papillomatosis"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. A laryngocele is dilation or herniation of the laryngeal saccule, and sustained raised intraluminal pressure from wind instrument playing is the classic association. The smooth dilation at the false cord is the characteristic finding, and computed tomography confirms it and defines the extent. — CMS I Neoplasms and Neck Masses, Slide 24"},{"q":"A 61-year-old man who smokes and drinks daily has a firm 3 centimetre neck mass present for six weeks. It is non-tender and barely mobile. There is no history of infection.","choices":["A two-week course of antibiotics","Excisional biopsy","Reassurance, as most neck masses are benign","Fine needle aspiration biopsy"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Six red flags are present at once: no infectious origin, duration over two weeks, size over 1.5 centimetres, firm and non-tender with little mobility, age over 40, and tobacco with alcohol. Aspiration separates neoplasm from inflammation and carcinoma from lymphoma without spilling tumour into the neck. — CMS I Neoplasms and Neck Masses, Slide 13"},{"q":"A 7-week-old has a firm, painless, discrete mass within the right sternocleidomastoid and holds his head tilted toward that side.","choices":["Immediate surgical excision","Fine needle aspiration to exclude malignancy","Observation with no intervention at all","Physical therapy, since most resolve spontaneously"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. The lesion is associated with congenital torticollis and follows a set course: it enlarges for two to three months and then regresses over four to eight, with about 80 per cent resolving. Physical therapy prevents a fixed restrictive torticollis while that happens, and surgery is reserved for persistent cases. — CMS I Neoplasms and Neck Masses, Slide 29"},{"q":"A wound in the posterior triangle has injured a nerve, and the patient now has a drooping, painful shoulder.","choices":["The phrenic nerve","The spinal accessory nerve","The vagus nerve","The hypoglossal nerve"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. The spinal accessory nerve crosses the muscular floor of the occipital triangle before passing deep to trapezius, which leaves it superficial and exposed. Denervating trapezius produces exactly the painful drooping shoulder described, and it is the classic injury of posterior triangle surgery. — CMS I Neoplasms and Neck Masses, Slide 5"},{"q":"A 9-year-old girl has a painless midline neck swelling noticed after a cold. It rises when she swallows and again when she sticks out her tongue.","choices":["Dermoid cyst","Branchial cleft cyst","Reactive lymph node","Thyroglossal duct cyst"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. The thyroid descends from the foramen caecum through the hyoid during development, and a persistent tract tethers the cyst to the hyoid bone. Protruding the tongue pulls the hyoid upward and the cyst with it, which is the pathognomonic sign and no other neck mass reproduces it. — CMS I Neoplasms and Neck Masses, Slide 23"},{"q":"A patient treated for medullary thyroid carcinoma is followed with a specific blood test for recurrence.","choices":["Parathyroid hormone","Calcitonin","Thyroglobulin","Thyroid-stimulating hormone"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Medullary carcinoma arises from parafollicular C cells, whose normal product is calcitonin, so the tumour secretes it and the level tracks disease burden. That makes it a specific marker for residual or recurrent disease, alongside external beam radiation for nodal disease and screening the family for multiple endocrine neoplasia. — CMS I Neoplasms and Neck Masses, Slide 44"},{"q":"An 18-month-old has a soft, doughy, non-tender, compressible mass in the posterior triangle. When a light is held against it the mass glows.","choices":["Lymphangioma","Haemangioma","Teratoma","Branchial cleft cyst"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Lymph spaces that fail to connect to the rest of the lymphatic system fill with clear lymph, and clear fluid transmits light. Positive transillumination is therefore the distinguishing bedside sign, and it also explains the soft, doughy, compressible texture. — CMS I Neoplasms and Neck Masses, Slide 26"},{"q":"A 27-year-old woman has a thyroid nodule. She had radiotherapy to the neck as a child for lymphoma.","choices":["Previous radiotherapy makes malignancy less likely","Her age of 27 makes malignancy essentially impossible","Radiotherapy history is relevant only for lymphoma recurrence","Her childhood irradiation raises the risk of thyroid malignancy"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Childhood head and neck irradiation is on the risk list for thyroid cancer, alongside age under 30 or over 60, full body irradiation for bone marrow transplantation, family history of thyroid cancer, and multiple endocrine neoplasia type 2. It changes the threshold for investigating a nodule rather than the method. — CMS I Neoplasms and Neck Masses, Slide 40"},{"q":"A 4-year-old has a midline neck mass that becomes red and tender after every cold, then settles.","choices":["Thyroglossal duct cyst","Branchial cleft cyst","Reactive lymph node","Lymphangioma"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Thyroglossal duct cysts are the commonest congenital neck mass at about a third of them, and they characteristically become apparent when infected after an upper respiratory infection. Midline position is the first branch of the congenital differential, and the tongue protrusion test confirms it. — CMS I Neoplasms and Neck Masses, Slide 23"},{"q":"A 30-year-old woman has tender cervical nodes, fever and myalgias. She eats rare steak and has an outdoor cat.","choices":["Brucellosis","Tularemia","Toxoplasmosis","Cat scratch disease"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Toxoplasma gondii is contracted through poorly cooked meat or ingestion of oocysts in cat faeces, and both exposures are present. Patients have fever, malaise, sore throat and myalgias with the adenopathy, diagnosis is serologic, and treatment is sulfonamides or pyrimethamine. — CMS I Neoplasms and Neck Masses, Slide 33"},{"q":"A newborn has a large firm neck mass present at delivery. Imaging shows calcification within it and the baby has stridor.","choices":["Thyroglossal duct cyst","Teratoma","Lymphangioma","Haemangioma"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Head and neck teratomas account for about 3.5 per cent of all teratomas, originate from pluripotent cells and are usually noted at birth or in the first year. The mixed tissue types explain the calcification, and when large enough they cause respiratory compromise or dysphagia, so excision is needed. — CMS I Neoplasms and Neck Masses, Slide 28"},{"q":"A 34-year-old man with untreated HIV has generalised cervical lymphadenopathy. Investigation finds no infectious or neoplastic cause.","choices":["Kaposi sarcoma","Tuberculous adenitis","Persistent generalised lymphadenopathy, treated by treating the HIV","Idiopathic follicular hyperplasia requiring excision"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Persistent generalised lymphadenopathy is defined by the absence of an identifiable infectious or neoplastic cause, and the neck is its commonest site. It reflects the immune activation of untreated infection, so the treatment is antiretroviral therapy rather than anything aimed at the nodes. — CMS I Neoplasms and Neck Masses, Slide 32"},{"q":"A 57-year-old woman on immunosuppression after a transplant has persistent cervical lymphadenopathy. Bacterial cultures are negative and antibiotics have failed.","choices":["Send fungal cultures and serology","Broaden the antibiotic spectrum","Reduce the immunosuppression and observe","Start empirical antituberculous therapy"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Immunocompromised patients are particularly susceptible to Candida, Histoplasma and Aspergillus, and both fungal cultures AND serology are required, because they are not found unless specifically sought. Negative bacterial cultures with antibiotic failure in this host is exactly the setting to look. — CMS I Neoplasms and Neck Masses, Slide 35"},{"q":"A 55-year-old man has had a firm neck lump for three days after a heavy cold, with a sore throat and mild fever.","choices":["Urgent referral for suspected malignancy","Excisional biopsy","Treat the infection and reassess in two weeks","Fine needle aspiration today"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Three days with an obvious infective context sits inside the window where reactive adenopathy is expected, and reactive nodes regress in one to two weeks. Duration over two weeks is what appears on the malignancy list, so the appropriate move is to treat what is there and reassess at that threshold. — CMS I Neoplasms and Neck Masses, Slide 13"},{"q":"A 58-year-old man with a neck mass reports night sweats, weight loss, hoarseness and dysphagia.","choices":["These symptoms suggest a benign lipoma","These are the general symptoms that accompany a concerning neck mass","These symptoms indicate a congenital cyst","These symptoms are typical of reactive adenopathy"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. The general clinical presentation listed includes fever, postnasal drip, rhinorrhoea, sore throat, otalgia, night sweats, weight loss, malaise, dysphagia and hoarseness. Hoarseness and dysphagia in particular suggest involvement of the upper aerodigestive tract. — CMS I Neoplasms and Neck Masses, Slide 15"},{"q":"A 64-year-old smoker has a firm cervical node. Aspiration shows squamous cell carcinoma, but no primary is obvious on first inspection.","choices":["Examine every mucosal surface of the head and neck","Proceed directly to neck dissection","Repeat the aspiration","Empirical radiotherapy to the neck"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Squamous carcinoma is the commonest metastatic lesion to the neck and comes from the skin or upper aerodigestive tract. A systematic office examination of all mucosal surfaces, the thyroid, the salivary glands and the skin usually finds the primary, and the primary determines both the surgery and the radiotherapy field. — CMS I Neoplasms and Neck Masses, Slide 39"},{"q":"A 47-year-old man has a persistent neck mass. The clinician wants to know whether it is solid or cystic and whether there is metastatic disease.","choices":["Plain radiography","Ultrasound alone","Magnetic resonance imaging as first line","Contrast computed tomography"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Contrast computed tomography differentiates vascularity, separates solid from cystic, evaluates for metastatic disease, and contributes to staging. It is the workhorse for a neck mass, with the single caveat that iodine contrast is avoided if thyroid cancer is suspected. — CMS I Neoplasms and Neck Masses, Slide 18"},{"q":"A 26-year-old woman has a slow-growing painless submental swelling. Imaging shows it arising from the sublingual gland and extending through mylohyoid into the neck.","choices":["Aspiration of the contents","Excision of the submandibular gland","Excision of the sublingual gland","Marsupialisation of the cyst"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. A ranula is a mucous retention cyst from the sublingual gland, called plunging when it herniates through mylohyoid. Removing the cyst alone leaves the gland producing the mucus, so it simply refills; excising the source gland is what prevents recurrence. — CMS I Neoplasms and Neck Masses, Slide 25"},{"q":"A 46-year-old man has a 1.4 centimetre thyroid nodule found incidentally on imaging done for another reason.","choices":["Nodules over 1 centimetre found incidentally warrant evaluation","Incidental nodules never require follow-up","Only nodules over 4 centimetres are evaluated","Evaluation is needed only if the patient is hyperthyroid"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Incidental nodules greater than 1 centimetre should be evaluated further, and this one exceeds that. Evaluation means ultrasound with fine needle aspiration plus thyroid-stimulating hormone and thyroid hormones, with recent growth, dysphagia or obstruction as the concerning symptoms to ask about. — CMS I Neoplasms and Neck Masses, Slide 40"},{"q":"A 50-year-old woman has a slowly enlarging lateral neck mass. On auscultation there is a bruit, and it is pulsatile.","choices":["Metastatic node","Paraganglioma","Schwannoma","Lipoma"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Paragangliomas head the benign vascular list, arising at the carotid body, the vagus or the jugulotympanic region. Their vascularity produces both the pulsation and the bruit, and the practical consequence is angiography before anything is put into the mass. — CMS I Neoplasms and Neck Masses, Slide 38"},{"q":"A 52-year-old woman has an immobile midline neck mass that rises when she swallows.","choices":["A branchial cleft cyst","A thyroglossal duct cyst","The thyroid","A lymph node"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. The thyroid is bound to the larynx and trachea by the pretracheal fascia, so it rises when the larynx rises during a swallow. That movement identifies the gland as the source and separates it from a node or cyst that stays still, and it should prompt ultrasound with aspiration, thyroid-stimulating hormone and T3 and T4. — CMS I Neoplasms and Neck Masses, Slide 40"},{"q":"A 50-year-old woman has a solitary thyroid nodule. Her thyroid-stimulating hormone is suppressed and she is mildly hyperthyroid.","choices":["Start antithyroid medication and review in six months","Radionuclide scan before any biopsy","Immediate fine needle aspiration","Contrast computed tomography of the neck"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. A suppressed thyroid-stimulating hormone raises the possibility of an autonomously functioning nodule. The scan separates a hot nodule, which carries a low malignant risk and needs no biopsy, from a cold or warm one, which does. Scanning first avoids an unnecessary aspiration. — CMS I Neoplasms and Neck Masses, Slide 41"},{"q":"A 41-year-old man who hunts rabbits has fever, chills, headache and fatigue with tonsillitis and painful cervical adenopathy.","choices":["Azithromycin","Sulfonamides or pyrimethamine","Streptomycin","Trimethoprim-sulfamethoxazole"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Rabbits, ticks or contaminated water point to Francisella tularensis, and tonsillitis with painful adenopathy and systemic symptoms is the oropharyngeal form. Diagnosis is by serology and culture, and streptomycin is the named treatment. — CMS I Neoplasms and Neck Masses, Slide 33"}]};
